= Deviations of the Haskell node <sec-deviations>

What the Haskell cardano-node emits differs from this specification in the following places. Implementations SHOULD follow the specification, not the deviation. Consumers of Haskell node logs have to accept the deviating form.

== Syntax

#table(
  columns: (auto, 1fr, 1fr, 0.8fr),
  table.header[\#][Where][Haskell node emits][Specification],
  [S1], [Point at genesis, e.g. `BlockContext.tip`, `ServerHeader.Update.rollBackTo`], [`"genesis (origin)"`], [`"genesis"`],
  [S2], [`TookSnapshot.tip`], [Haskell `show` output: `"RealPoint (SlotNo 981) 4c9e24c2…"`], [Point: `"4c9e24c2…@981"`],
  [S3], [ChainHash at genesis: `ForgedBlock.blockPrev`, `SendFetchRequest.head`, `Server.SendBlock.block`, `ForgingMempoolSnapshot.mempoolHash`], [`"GenesisHash"`], [`"genesis"`],
  [S4], [ChainHash at genesis: `CompletedBlockFetch.block`], [`"Genesis"`], [`"genesis"`],
  [S5], [`BlockContext`: slot], [key `"current slot"` (contains a space)], [key `slot`],
  [S6], [`BlockContext`: number of the block to be forged], [key `"tipBlockNo"`], [key `blockNo`, the number of the block to be forged],
  [S7], [`BlockContext`: point extended], [key `tip`], [key `prev`, as in `ForgeTickedLedgerState`],
  [S8], [TxId in `Mempool.*` events], [truncated to 8 hex characters (`"a2f68ff4"`)], [full TxId],
)

== Semantics

#table(
  columns: (auto, 2fr, 1fr),
  table.header[\#][Haskell node behaviour][Specification],
  [E1], [`TxInbound.Collected.count` is the size of the peer's latest reply, not of the submitted batch, so `Processed.rejected` (computed as `count − accepted`) is wrong whenever the two differ, and can be negative.], [`count` is the size of the submitted batch; `rejected`, if emitted, equals `count − accepted` (@sec-txs).],
)

// ----- 9 -------------------------------------------------------------

= Open questions <sec-open>

+ *Leios.* Leios introduces new observables (endorser blocks, votes, certificates), and changes what "adoption" means. A future version of this specification extends the catalogue once the Leios traces are stable.
+ *Versioned schemata.* Currently there's one schema, and supporting it is all an implementation needs for its performance to be analysed. Once the catalogue evolves (e.g. for Leios), the schema becomes versioned: a stream would then declare the version it conforms to (e.g. in a start-up event), so consumers can select the matching schema, and the documented deviations of a given implementation version.

